1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. Qc2 d5 5. e3 O-O 6. Nf3 Nbd7 7. Bd3 Qe7 8. O-O dxc4 9. Bxc4 e5 10. Nd5 Nxd5 11. Bxd5 Nf6 12. Bb3 e4 13. Nd2 Bd6 14. Re1 Ng4 15. h3 Nf6 16. Nc4 Be6 17. Nxd6 cxd6 18. Bxe6 Qxe6 19. Bd2 Rfc8 20. Qa4 Nd5 21. Rec1 a6 22. Qb3 b5 23. Rxc8+ Rxc8 24. Rc1 Rxc1+ 25. Bxc1 {Sources: L'Échiquier 1929 No.2; StarBase 4.56. The match was announced in BCM, January 1929, thus: "A match will be played in London early in January between E. Colle, Belgian Champion, and W. Winter." BCM had nothing further to say; the Exeter and Plymouth Gazette - Thursday 07 February 1929 - reported that the match ended in a 5-5 draw, both players having won two games, with six draws. The Yorkshire Post, 23 January 1929, reported that 'the chess match played at the Imperial Chess Club, London, between Colle, the Belgian champion, and W. Winter, one of the strongest London players, was declared a draw yesterday by mutual consent after the tenth game. Each player had scored two wins, and six games were drawn. The second and eighth games were won by Colle, and the fifth and tenth (and last) game by Winter.' Additional information: the Nieuwe Rotterdamsche Courant for Saturday 12 January 1929 said that the match had been going 'since last Thursday' so the first game must have been played on 10 January 1929. We have a date of 15 January 1929 for the fifth game, so it seems reasonable to assume that intervening games were played on 11, 12, 13 January with Sunday 14 January being a free day.} 1/2-1/2

